  "query_preview": "..\n\nBlocking pattern still in place across the conus with the Great \nLakes locked under the stacked ridging. Local weather stays quiet \nthrough Tuesday with the ridge in place but the ridge will begin to \nslowly shift eastward as the blocking low off the east coast \nreleases allowing for a more active mid week stretch. \n\nCenter of the surface ridge passes over lower MI tonight leading to \nanother calm and clear night with varying degrees of fog across the \narea. Any fog will again burn off quickly in the morning as temps \nonce again rise into the 70s. Southwesterly winds return on Tuesday \nas the center of the high drifts off to the east while the \npositively tilted mid/upper level trough folds over the region. This \nwill allow for a boost in high temps by a couple degrees into the \nmid 70s with the added warm advection. 850mb temps should increase \nfrom the current upper single digits, into the mid teens. Though \nhigh temps mid week will be tempered by the incoming clouds and \nrain, we will see a boost in low temps Wednesday night with temps \nholding in the mid 60s. \n\nAs for the aforementioned clouds and rain, a frontal boundary will \nstretch from the broad parent low over central Canada down through \nthe Midwest and southward to a cutoff low and surface reflection \nover the four corners. The front will stall mid week just to our \nwest before slowly pushing east of the area Thursday night. This \nwill lead to a long duration light rainfall event with periods of \nmoderate rain associated with any mid level waves riding up along \nthe front late Wednesday into Thursday night. The cutoff low will \nabsorb remnants of a Pacific tropical system adding high moisture \ncontent to the airmass with a strong southwesterly low level jet \nushering some of this moisture into the region. Higher rainfall \ntotals will occur west of SE MI, with the front becoming more \nprogressive once it reaches us which will limit totals somewhat \nlocally. At this point we're still looking for around a half inch to \nthree quarters of an inch of rainfall over the 36-48 hour window. \nThere remains a low chance of some embedded thunder over the area \nmainly Thursday when a strong 50 knot low level jet pushes a mid \nlevel wave just south of the area with a theta e plume surging \ninto SE MI which may be enough forcing to allow some convection. \n\nFriday we could be dealing with some post frontal stratiform light \nrain depending on how fast the incoming surface high can push into \nthe area. Regardless, we are in for a cool weekend as the thermal \ntrough sets up and sits over the region. High temps will only reach \ninto the 60s with lows down into the low 40s.",
